<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vallabhbhai Patel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sardar Patel, Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vallabhbhai Patel, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardar</a> Patel, was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> leader and the first Deputy Prime Minister and Home Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, from 1947 until his death in 1950. A lawyer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, he led peasant campaigns such as the Bardoli Satyagraha of 1928 and became a senior figure in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. As minister for the states he oversaw the integration of more than five hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> into the Indian Union.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel.jpg" alt="Vallabhbhai Patel" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of India work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sardar_patel_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vallabhbhai Jhaverbhai Patel, one of the six children of Jhaverbhai Patel and Ladba, was born in Nadiad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and raised in the Gujarati countryside. His date of birth was never officially recorded; he entered 31 October on his matriculation examination papers. He belonged to the Patidars, specifically the Leva Patel community of central Gujarat, and he became a successful lawyer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In September 1917 Patel spoke at Borsad in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>'s petition demanding swaraj, and a month later he met Gandhi for the first time. He became secretary of the Gujarat Sabha, fought the forced servitude known as veth and organised relief after plague and famine in Kheda. When the Kheda peasants' plea for exemption from taxation was refused, Patel volunteered to lead the struggle.</p><p>He toured the villages and urged complete unity and non-violence, and when tax revenue was withheld and the government sent police to seize property, he organised volunteers to help villagers hide valuables. The government agreed to suspend taxes for a year and scaled back the rate. In 1920 Patel was elected president of the Gujarat Pradesh Congress Committee, which he led until 1945.</p><p>From 1934 he was the Congress's main fundraiser and chairman of its Central Parliamentary Board. He was arrested in 1940 and imprisoned for nine months, and he opposed the Cripps mission of 1942. In 1946, realising that rejecting the Cabinet Mission plan of 16 May would leave only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> to form a government, he lobbied the Congress Working Committee to accept it.</p><p>As the first Home Minister he organised relief for partition refugees, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/v-p-menon">V. P. Menon</a> he lobbied the princes from 6 August 1947, offering favourable terms such as privy purses, while not ruling out force. All but three of the 565 states acceded willingly; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, Junagadh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> did not. He was also a senior figure in the Constituent Assembly and is remembered as a founder of the All India Services.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Patel sparred with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and the states, but on 30 January 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> told him not to leave the government, and he was the last man to speak with Gandhi privately before his assassination. His health declined through 1949 and he died on 15 December 1950. His commitment to national integration earned him the name Iron Man of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Statue of Unity, about 182 metres (597 ft) high, was dedicated to him on 31 October 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vallabhbhai Patel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their rulers had signed Instruments of Accession surrendering defence, foreign affairs and communications to the Dominion of India, but the States Ministry under <b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b>, with its secretary V.P. Menon, judged most of them too small to survive as separate units and pressed them to merge.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b> (1875–1950), who led the peasants of Bardoli in 1928, was its great organiser; Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, an Islamic scholar and journalist, was its president from 1940 to 1946; and the poet Sarojini Naidu became the first Indian woman to preside over it, in 1925.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b>, Rajendra Prasad and Anugrah Narayan Sinha supported it openly, as did Asoka Mehta, Jayaprakash Narayan and many veteran Gandhians and socialists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quit-india-movement">Quit India movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vallabhbhai Patel</b> and V. P. Menon integrated more than five hundred princely states, using force against Hyderabad in 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a></li><li><blockquote>In early 1948, under pressure from V. P. Menon and <b>Sardar Patel</b>, the rulers agreed to merge their states into the United State of Kathiawar, inaugurated in February 1948; Junagadh State joined in 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/saurashtra-state">Saurashtra State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nadiad, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.7,72.87&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Nadiad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Vallabhbhai Patel"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6007 5566h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6007 5566h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.7,72.87&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
